### Analysis
The corruption pattern you posted is beautiful because it perfectly diagnoses the issue. 
Notice how `ESP-ROM:esp32s3-20210725` became `ESP-ROM:s3s32213`. It prints exactly 7 characters perfectly, then drops 2, prints 2, drops 1.

**Why?** 
Because `LPUART1` is receiving data at 115200 baud, and `LPUART0` (your debug console) is transmitting data via `PRINTF` at 115200 baud. Mathematically, sending a byte takes the exact same amount of time as receiving one. However, the software overhead of executing the `PRINTF` function makes it take *just a fraction longer*. So, every 7th or 8th byte, the MCXA falls 1 full byte behind, the hardware buffer overflows, our new error-clearing logic silently wipes the fault so it doesn't lock up forever, but the byte that arrived during the overflow is permanently lost!

**Why it "didn't work before anyway":** 
Before we added the error-clearing logic, the very first time the ESP32 sent a burst (like its bootloader log) and caused a single microsecond of jitter, the UART hardware locked up forever. Now it doesn't lock up, it just drops bytes because `PRINTF` is physically choking it. 

We will remove `PRINTF` and upgrade the polling architecture to drain the buffer in a `while` loop so it can process incoming bursts at lightning speed.

